  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nIt's a mostly clear and tranquil morning, with a few high clouds \nfloating by and a few low clouds off the coast. A low pressure \ntrough zipping west to east through northern CA will bring more \nhigh clouds today and more low clouds tonight into Wednesday. In\nfact, the low clouds could generate a few light showers in \nsouthwest San Diego County this evening through about sunrise \nWednesday. This trough will also enhance onshore winds in the \ncoastal waters, the mountains, and the deserts mainly this \nevening. Isolated strongest gusts should reach around 35-40 mph in\nthe mountains and deserts. The trough exits to the east but it is\nfollowed by only timid high pressure and some very weak offshore \nflow. A high pressure ridge over the West Coast amplifies on \nFriday and Saturday. This will boost temperatures by virtue of the\nhigher pressure, but also from stronger offshore flow, which will\nfavor the warming closer to the coast. That offshore flow will \nproduce some winds, maybe some legit Santa Ana winds, in foothills\nand some adjacent valleys. Offshore winds right now look most \nlikely and strongest on Saturday and Sunday mornings, when top \ngusts could reach 40 mph. This is a rough estimate for now, and \nthe wind picture will become clearer in coming days. High \ntemperatures Saturday and Sunday near the coast and in western \nvalleys will soar well into the 80s, about 5-15 degrees above \nnormal. That will produce a moderate HeatRisk in those areas. \nHowever, the air will be dry, allowing for efficient cooling \nduring the long nights, especially where winds calm. All this will\nmitigate the heat risks. The ridge aloft and the offshore flow \nbegin to weaken by Monday as a strong jet drives a longwave trough\neastward across the Pacific.",
